cartridge "OIL DEFENSE/Mark H. Thomas/1988"

' OIL DEFENSE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Oil Defense", COMPUTE!'s Gazette March 1988
' (Commodore 64 machine language, 3,867 bytes at $0801 started by SYS 2062;
' the disk and the program name no author). Made for Amy Studio by Claude
' (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/oildef.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: a night refinery town, three oil tanks with gauge
' windows that drain as you watch, a smooth green launcher, saboteur
' drones, rockets, bursts and sounds.
'
' The game: saboteur drones drift over the town and drain any oil tank
' they touch. Slide the launcher along the ground and fire rockets
' straight up to destroy them. Destroy the wave's drones (10, then 5 more
' each wave) to clear it. When two tanks are dry the game is over.
'
' From the original: the launcher moves left and right along the bottom
' (sprite 0) and fires one rocket at a time straight up from its middle,
' the button held fires again as soon as the rocket is gone (sprite 1);
' three drones (sprites 5-7) wander: each axis keeps a direction (still,
' one way or the other) and picks a new one at random now and then; a
' drone touching a tank drains it, a little every 10 steps; a rocket that
' hits a drone scores 999 (BCD add of $0999, eight digits shown), the drone
' bursts and comes back from the top while more remain than are on
' screen; the counter of drones left starts at 10 and each new wave has 5
' more; the tanks are not refilled between waves; the game ended when all
' three tanks were empty.
'
' Liberties: the C64's drones wrapped round the screen's sides and could
' fly anywhere: here they stay inside the TV-safe field (columns 2-29),
' bounce at its sides and at the tank tops, and lean downward (two of the
' four vertical choices go down) so they come for the oil (the C64's
' tank damage itself was buggy: it cleared bytes past the tank sprite);
' each tank holds 24 units and loses one every 20 frames while a drone
' touches it; the game ends when two tanks are dry (the C64 waited for
' all three, and standing still under one tank kept it full for ever);
' a cleared wave pumps 6 units back into each tank (the C64 never
' refilled them); drones start off drifting sideways; drone speed rises
' each wave (the C64 sped the launcher up with time and slowed it when it touched the scenery; here it moves 2
' pixels a frame always); WAVE CLEARED and a jingle between waves; a title,
' credits and GAME OVER page (the C64 restarted on a key).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the launcher, a button
' fires. On the title, up and down choose START / HOW TO PLAY (two pages
' of rules) / CREDITS, a button chooses; the cursor starts on START.

